><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study provides an injury and illness profile for sailing athletes&#xD;across the development pathway through to Olympic class athletes.&#xD;• The majority of injuries and illnesses reported by Olympic class and&#xD;SSPP sailing athletes did not result in time loss from training or competition.&#xD;• The body site with the greatest injury burden was the shoulder and&#xD;respiratory infections were the most frequently reported illness.&#xD;• There was no difference in the injury incidence rate between sexes,&#xD;however, female athletes were found to have a </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>3.6-fold greater rate&#xD;of reported illness compared to male athletes.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
